---
export const title = "My Self Hosting Setup";
export const date = "2026-10-15";
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
---

<BlogLayout title={title} date={date}>


  <h1>START SELFHOSTING NOW</h1>

  <p>
    In this entry I will be showcasing all the stuff I self host and how
    (kinda)! I'm not super well versed in this area but I want to show that you
    don't need good hardware or smarts to be able to setup and run useful stuff.
    If I can do it, ou can! Also it's been months since I touched the config /
    setup side of anything so please expect some misinfo.
  

    <br /><br />

    <span style="color:#333636;">────────────────────────────</span>

    <br />

    <h3>HARDWARE</h3>

    A 7 year old hp laptop with an i3 processor 4gb ram. Not a whole lot but it gets the job done.

    <br /> <br />

    <div class ="image">

      <img src ="https://file.garden/akY52Mebcxapr9RG/misc/blog_artlog/blog-laptop.jpg"  height="500px">

    </div>

    <br />

    If you are in any way serious about this I highly recommend actually buying
    stuff but if you're like me and just want to mess around, you really don't
    need much.

    <br /><br />

    <span style="color:#333636;">────────────────────────────</span>

    <br />

    <h3>SOFTWARE</h3>
    
    My server currently runs linux mint because I read the RAM usage wasn't too
    crazy. If you are only familiar with windows I recommend it, it's very
    similar and easy to use. Definitely look into other options though because
    there's so many other distros and stuff like Proxmoxwhich is probably way
    better. You can use windows or mac but as far as i know with docker anyway
    linux just makes it easier.
    <br /><br />

    To run and deploy my apps I use docker. You kinda configure stuff in
    dockerfile (you can copy paste nearly all of it) run a few commands and
    you're set.
    <br /><br />

    I like to use a web gui (?) with it called Portainer. This way you can
    compose and deploy stuff from your browser (on any device as long as you're
    on same network/ vpnning / portforwarding) and also see the status of all
    the containers you got running.
    <br /><br />

    <div class ="image">

      <img src ="https://file.garden/akY52Mebcxapr9RG/misc/blog_artlog/blog-portainer.png" height="500px">

    </div>

    <br />

    I like to use a VPN called Tailscale to be able to access my apps
    (services?) from outside my home network. You create an account and login on
    all your other devices and boom you got a Tailnet. There are other vpn stuff
    or you could like open some ports on your router maybe (bad idea) but yeah
    this is simple and it works. Also my mobile data on my phone won't work
    unless I connect to it which is strange so god bless tailscale.
    

    <br /><br />

    <span style="color:#333636;">────────────────────────────</span>

    <br />

    <h3>WHAT I RUN</h3>

    <h4>Live Sync for Obsidian</h4>

    This might be my most used (using it to draft this blog rn) because I love a notes app, and not
    having to pay 7 euro a month is pretty neat. You have to set up a database with CouchDB or the other options, 
    install an extension in obsidian and then go
    through a setup thing. I had a few issues when I first started with stuff
    going out of sync, but turns out you just need to set the syncing option to
    LiveSync on all your devices and it's chill idk why it's not the default.
    <br /><br />

    <div class ="image">

      <img src ="https://file.garden/akY52Mebcxapr9RG/misc/blog_artlog/blog-obsidian.png" height="500px" >

    </div>


    <h4>Jellyfin</h4>

    This is a media streaming service. It's mainly for videos but it
    works grand for music and I think they have ebook features as well? For the
    video stuff you probably want halfway decent specs and wifi for it to be
    worth using but if you have similar stuff to me, it still absolutely works.
    And i've had zero issues with music. 
    <br /><br />


    <div class ="image">

      <img src ="https://file.garden/akY52Mebcxapr9RG/misc/blog_artlog/blog-jellyfin.png" height="500px" >

    </div>


    <h4>Seafile </h4>

    This is a file hosting thing like google drive. I actually recommend NextCloud instead but I think I had
    some issues accessing it from my other devices so I switched to this. Works
    grand but I have to use the website instead of a more convenient client on
    mobile. Also haven't noticed any performance issues despite my laptop.

    <h4>Qbittorent WebUI and Filebrowser</h4>

    I use these together with Jellyfin to start downloading a show/movie/album, then move said download into Jellyfin's
    media folder so I can start streaming. Makes it super easy to expand and
    manage my library. 
    <br /><br />

    <div class ="image">

      <img src ="https://file.garden/akY52Mebcxapr9RG/misc/blog_artlog/blog-qbittorent.png" height="500px" >

    </div>


    <br /><br />

    <span style="color:#333636;">────────────────────────────</span>

    <br />
    
